{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they journeyed from Rephidim, and they came to the wilderness of Sinai, and they encamped in the wilderness, and Israel encamped there opposite the mountain.</b> See how many duplications are written here. What would the verse have lacked had it said simply, 'And they journeyed from Rephidim and encamped in the wilderness of Sinai opposite the mountain'? And why did it first say 'and they encamped' (plural) and afterward 'and he encamped' (singular)? First it says 'the wilderness of Sinai' and afterward it says simply 'in the wilderness,' and first it calls the mountain by the name 'Sinai' and afterward calls it simply 'the mountain.' And it appears that all of this is proof that Israel were not fit to receive the Torah until there would be peace among them — 'the words of the wise are as goads, and as nails well fastened are those of the masters of assemblies, given from one Shepherd' (Ecclesiastes 12:11) — and all the paths of Torah are peace. For through division of hearts this one prohibits and that one permits, and the Torah is found to be like two Torot. This is what it says: 'In the third month… on this day they came to the wilderness of Sinai' (Exodus 19:1). It indicates that this month, whose sign is Gemini, which signifies cleaving and love — that together they would be whole, as twins to one another, and then there would be abundant peace for those who love the Torah. And there is in this an allusion as well to the two tablets, which were like twins together, five opposite five." "And afterward it says, 'And they journeyed from Rephidim' — that is, from the place where they were in strife and division, for that place was called Massah and Meribah, and 'Rephidim' is letters of 'פְּרוּדִים' (perudim, divisions), like 'כֶּשֶׂב כֶּבֶשׂ' (kesev keves), on account of the division that was among them. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 106a) interpreted it from the language of 'רַף יָדַיִם' (raf yadayim, slackening of hands), and this depends on that: for through the division that was among them, their hands were slackened from the Torah. And now 'they journeyed from Rephidim' — from that place, that is to say, they removed themselves from division — 'and they came to the wilderness of Sinai.' For this place caused them to be in a single bond, for the pursuit of honor and dominion is the cause of every quarrel and every affliction. And through seeing that Mount Sinai, the lowest of the mountains, was the mountain Elohim desired for His dwelling, they then saw that the Holy One, blessed be He, chooses the humble, and through this they chose the trait of submission, and this is the cause of peace. Therefore it mentions them in plural language — 'and they came to the wilderness of Sinai and they encamped in the wilderness' — to indicate that from the side of the wilderness they were still divided in their opinions and were not yet of one heart like one man. But when they came opposite the mountain and it was told to them that upon this mountain HaShem would descend — then 'Israel encamped there' (singular): they were of one heart like one man. But at first, before they came to this mountain — whether in their coming into the wilderness or in their encamping in the wilderness — they had not yet departed from their division, until they came opposite the mountain as stated. For its name indicates the opposite, namely hatred, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 89a): 'Sinai, for from there descended hatred upon the nations,' etc. Therefore it says, 'And Israel encamped there opposite the mountain' — for the essence of the mountain caused the peace, and not its name."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:19.807307298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}